import{_ as t,o as a,c as n,a0 as i}from"./chunks/framework.DzvPB4Y-.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Demand Units and Business Rules","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"frameworks/csp1d/business-scenario.md","filePath":"frameworks/csp1d/business-scenario.md"}'),s={name:"frameworks/csp1d/business-scenario.md"};function o(r,e,d,l,c,h){return a(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="demand-units-and-business-rules" tabindex="-1">Demand Units and Business Rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#demand-units-and-business-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Demand Units and Business Rules&quot;">​</a></h1><p>After <a href="./first-slitting.html">your first slitting plan</a>, specify what the customer orders before adding machines and compatibility rules. Demand labels, physical units, per-use contributions and plan usage must form one consistent chain.</p><h2 id="_1-start-with-an-algorithm-independent-acceptance-case" tabindex="-1">1. Start with an Algorithm-Independent Acceptance Case <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-start-with-an-algorithm-independent-acceptance-case" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Start with an Algorithm-Independent Acceptance Case&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Suppose validated plan P produces two rolls of A per use, each weighing 10 kg. This is a business calculation, not an input protocol where the framework guesses the unit of a bare number:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>P contribution per use</th><th style="text-align:right;">Required uses</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A, six rolls</td><td>Two rolls</td><td style="text-align:right;">3</td><td>Six rolls</td></tr><tr><td>A, 60 kg</td><td>20 kg</td><td style="text-align:right;">3</td><td>60 kg</td></tr><tr><td>A, 30 kg</td><td>20 kg</td><td style="text-align:right;">Not exactly satisfiable</td><td>Explicit overproduction or another plan required</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Do not silently use an indivisible plan 1.5 times. With modeled overproduction, two uses can deliver 40 kg; with exact production and no other plan, the problem is infeasible. The allowance belongs in the model, not just a changed order label.</p><h2 id="_2-express-orders-with-actual-demand-types" tabindex="-1">2. Express Orders with Actual Demand Types <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-express-orders-with-actual-demand-types" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Express Orders with Actual Demand Types&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Demand</th><th>Kotlin entry</th><th>Rust entry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legacy roll count</td><td><code>ProductDemand.legacyRoll</code></td><td><code>ProductDemand::legacy_roll</code></td></tr><tr><td>Legacy sheet count</td><td><code>ProductDemand.legacySheet</code></td><td><code>ProductDemand::legacy_sheet</code></td></tr><tr><td>Legacy weight</td><td><code>ProductDemand.legacyWeight</code></td><td><code>ProductDemand::legacy_weight</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>These helpers express demand conventions; they do not invent product weight, length or unit-weight data. For quantity constructors, discrete/continuous behavior also follows the actual unit, not only <code>mode</code>.</p><p>Use the first-lesson input and solve entry and read contributions in the actual demand unit. Legacy mode labels do not override quantities or production-density data.</p><h2 id="_3-establish-contributions-before-solving" tabindex="-1">3. Establish Contributions Before Solving <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-establish-contributions-before-solving" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Establish Contributions Before Solving&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Identify demands by stable product IDs, retaining width, optional length and weight sources.</li><li>Check <code>CuttingPlanDemandContribution</code> quantities against order units. A roll order still counts rolls when the product also has weight.</li><li>Use the language-specific contribution construction/calculation path rather than treating slice counts as kilograms. Verify the dimensions of width, length, areal density or per-piece mass.</li><li>Recompute each product&#39;s output as <code>Σ contribution × usage</code>, with separate surplus, shortage and allowed bounds.</li></ol><p>Whether two measurement requirements for the same product must both hold is a business decision. An adapter must not overwrite an order merely because the product ID matches.</p><h2 id="_4-put-machine-rules-in-the-right-layer" tabindex="-1">4. Put Machine Rules in the Right Layer <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-put-machine-rules-in-the-right-layer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Put Machine Rules in the Right Layer&quot;">​</a></h2><p>“Material M cannot run on machine X” can be checked per plan. “Machine X can process at most 100 m this shift” needs a sum across plans; checking each plan separately against 100 is insufficient.</p><p>Cross-plan rules belong in the relevant context/modeling pipelines. With column generation, inspect LP, dual/pricing and final participation too. Removing offending plans after solving does not preserve demand coverage.</p><h2 id="_5-retain-three-assertion-groups" tabindex="-1">5. Retain Three Assertion Groups <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-retain-three-assertion-groups" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Retain Three Assertion Groups&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Changing demand mode while retaining the numeric amount changes units and contribution calculations, not just a label on the same output.</li><li>A prohibited material-machine pair cannot enter selection. Prohibiting all feasible pairs leaves no deliverable output.</li><li>Tightened aggregate capacity is checked across all plan usages, not independently per selected plan.</li></ul><p>Use the <a href="./../integration.html">integration guide</a> to distinguish normal no-solution outcomes, technical failure and native reports. Feasibility recovery or heuristic fallback retains its real status, not an automatic upgrade to optimality.</p><p>Repository regression commands live in <a href="./../verification.html">Verification</a>. Complete the business input, call and result checks before using them.</p>',20)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",o]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
